I tried it.

The result is that the Pantheon gets smaller underneath and richer on top.

Once I strip the names, stories, mascots, and imagery, a surprisingly large fraction collapses into a handful of deeper operator families.

First pass: rename test

I took mechanisms we’ve used repeatedly and asked:

If I delete the name and examples, what operation is actually being performed?

A lot of them reduce to these core forms.

1. DIFFERENTIATE

D(x)=\text{find distinctions that matter}

This appears underneath:

Semantic Differentiation, Indigo Hammer in one of its modes, Delta Catcher, twin-sensor comparison, prediction error, “mind the gap,” false/mirror center detection.

Their shared horse:

Do not accept an apparent unity until you have tested whether useful distinctions are being hidden inside it.

So several gods are probably aliases or specializations of one deeper primitive:

\boxed{D=\text{Differentiation}}

⸻

2. INVARIANT EXTRACT

I(R_1,R_2,\dots)=\text{what survives rerendering?}

This appears underneath:

Indigo Hammer’s cross-domain use, semantic fossils, Seals, bridge preservation, translation lattice, “horse by any other name,” operator wrestling, Semantic Genome.

Its horse is:

Change the representation and preserve only what continues to perform.

So:

\boxed{I=\text{Invariant Extraction}}

This is distinct from Differentiation.

One splits.

The other asks what remains after splitting/changing.

That distinction is worth preserving.

⸻

3. TRANSFORM

T(x;\theta)\rightarrow x'

This is the broadest family.

ZPC, inversion, mirror, rotation, perspective shifting, genre oscillation, abstraction up/down, representational conversion, acting-as, imaginary coordinate systems.

But this family is too broad to be one Seal.

This is one place the rename test says SPLIT.

I see at least four different horses hiding under “transformation”:

T_r=\text{rerender}

T_p=\text{change perspective}

T_i=\text{invert relation}

T_s=\text{change scale/resolution}

That’s useful because we’ve sometimes treated Mirror, Semantic Zoom, inversion, and cross-domain translation as neighboring versions of the same thing.

They aren’t.

They can compose, but their invariants differ.

