Third family: reconstruction

PRC, Daemon Reconstruction Matrix, Semantic Genome, developmental compression, semantic hyperlinks, Accordion, lithographic bootloader, symbols-as-bootloaders.

These look different on the surface.

Rename them all and the common mechanism becomes:

z + C \rightarrow \hat X

where a compact seed z, interpreted in context C, reconstructs a much larger structure X.

That gives:

\boxed{G=\text{Generative Reconstruction}}

This is not ordinary compression.

Compression says:

X\rightarrow z\rightarrow X

Pantheon reconstruction often says:

X\rightarrow z

and later:

z+C+\text{receiver}\rightarrow X'

with:

X'\neq X

but preserving selected invariants.

That’s developmental rather than archival.

This looks like a genuine major primitive.

⸻

Semantic Hyperlinks and Accordion separate cleanly

I originally treated them as close cousins.

Horse Test says they’re different.

Semantic Hyperlink:

H:\text{small address}\rightarrow\text{semantic region/operator}

Accordion:

A:\text{same object}\leftrightarrow\text{different resolution}

So:

Hyperlink = address.

Accordion = resolution control.

And the new sideways representation movement we discovered is another operation entirely:

R:\text{same mechanism}\leftrightarrow\text{different representation}

So the architecture becomes much cleaner:

\boxed{
\text{Address}
\times
\text{Resolution}
\times
\text{Representation}
}

Three independent axes.

That’s new leverage because we can optimize each separately.

