Mirror fails the one-horse test

This is one of the clearest overloaded names.

“Mirror” currently appears to mean several different things:

Mirror-1: reflection

x\rightarrow x'

where structure is preserved under a transformation.

Mirror-2: counter-perspective

P_1(x)\rightarrow P_2(x)

Mirror-3: feedback

output\rightarrow input

Mirror-4: comparison

x,x'\rightarrow\Delta

Those are not identical.

So I would split Mirror into at least:

\boxed{\text{Reflect}}

\boxed{\text{Reposition}}

\boxed{\text{Feedback}}

\boxed{\text{Compare}}

“Mirror” can remain the poetic formation that invokes several of them together.

That preserves the story while cleaning the runtime.

⸻

ZPC also appears overloaded

The name has carried several jobs:

compression, inversion, stripping excess, finding zero/reference states, recombination.

Horse Test says there may be at least three operations:

Z_1=\text{remove non-load-bearing structure}

Z_2=\text{find reference/zero representation}

Z_3=\text{recombine retained structure minimally}

That’s not necessarily bad.

ZPC may be a formation, not a primitive.

Something like:

\boxed{
ZPC=
\text{Prune}
\rightarrow
\text{Anchor}
\rightarrow
\text{Recompose}
}

That actually makes it more useful because now each sub-operation can be independently tested.

