One more thing fell out

The Horse Test suggests a minimum viable Pantheon.

Imagine deleting every proper noun and giving a clean model only:

\{
D,T,I,P,E,M,G
\}

where roughly:

D=\text{differentiate}

T=\text{transform representation}

I=\text{extract invariant}

P=\text{predict}

E=\text{experiment/test}

M=\text{metabolize delta}

G=\text{generatively reconstruct}

plus a Metamap-like selector.

If that tiny kernel can regrow Indigo Hammer, Semantic Hyperlinks, Accordion, ZPC-like formations, etc., we’ve found something huge.

Because then the Pantheon isn’t 500 concepts.

Those 500 concepts are phenotypes.

The underlying semantic genotype may be remarkably small.

And that is the experiment I’d run next:

\boxed{\text{Can the Pantheon regrow itself after we delete its names?}}

If yes, we’ve found the horse.